Here is a breakdown of three of the most widely used algorithmic trading strategies, ranging from execution-focused to strategy-focused.
1. Volume Weighted Average Price (VWAP)
VWAP is an execution algorithm used primarily by institutional traders and mutual funds. Rather than trying to predict the market, its goal is to buy or sell a large block of shares without causing sudden price spikes.
The algorithm breaks a large order into smaller chunks and executes them throughout the day, aiming to keep the average purchase price as close as possible to the historical average for that timeframe.

$$VWAP = \frac{\sum (Price \times Volume)}{\sum Volume}$$
Key Insight: If a trading algorithm buys a million shares below the daily VWAP, it means the trader executed the block order at a "better than average" price for that specific day.
2. Statistical Arbitrage (Pairs Trading)
Statistical arbitrage relies on complex mathematical models to identify pricing inefficiencies between correlated assets. The most famous subset of this is Pairs Trading.
If two companies in the same sector (e.g., Coca-Cola and Pepsi) historically move together, the algorithm monitors the spread between their stock prices. If the spread widens beyond a specific statistical threshold (usually measured in standard deviations), the algorithm assumes the historical relationship will eventually revert to the mean.
The Execution: It automatically short-sells the overperforming stock and goes long (buys) the underperforming stock.
The Result: When the prices converge back to their historical average, the algorithm closes both positions for a profit, remaining market-neutral regardless of whether the broader stock market went up or down.
3. Moving Average Crossover (Momentum)
This is a classic trend-following algorithm that triggers automated trades based on overlapping timeframes. It relies on two moving averages: a "fast" one (e.g., a 50-day average) and a "slow" one (e.g., a 200-day average).
The algorithm constantly calculates these averages and looks for intersections:
Golden Cross (Buy Signal): The fast moving average crosses above the slow moving average, indicating a sudden surge in upward momentum.
Death Cross (Sell Signal): The fast moving average crosses below the slow moving average, indicating downward momentum.
